{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, the run-lifetime rule at the container that executes no statements at all. A queued `every` occurrence runs `once the handler is free` while the main line has not finished (spec/interaction-events.md:246-253), and an iteration of an EMPTY loop body is main-line progress just as much as one that runs a statement: it is charged against the execution budget on the same terms. A loop body's statements are what carry the boundary, so an empty body -- which has no statements -- had none. Measured before this was pinned: `forever [ ]` gave a queued occurrence three firings before `ol-limit` where `forever [ print 0 ]` gave eleven, so the ruling's back-to-back guarantee held only for loops that happened to contain something. This fixture uses a terminating `repeat 4 [ ]` so the stream is finite: the handler fires SEVEN times and the run ends cleanly. A runtime whose boundary is statement-only fires it three times here. The empty body is the whole point of the fixture and must not be `improved` into a non-empty one.",
